AUDIO: 'time to support me, and let\'s move on to the video. So let\'s be clear, because I\'m going to name a lot of letters and numbers now, and I don\'t want it to sound like a chaotic set of symbols. So, Minimax H3 is a video model that generates images and audio together in a'

[03:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'single pass, and it has different variants, where the name indicates the type of input data. FL2VA stands for "first and last frame in video and audio". You give it a starting image,'

[03:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'optionally an ending image, and it animates the transition between them, filling in the gaps. If you don\'t provide anything, it\'s just text in the video, and another model that Minimax provides is "ref to VA", meaning "reference in video and audio". So'

[03:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'instead of the initial frame with the video reference, you pass in a photo of a person, a character sheet, or multiple photos and say, "this person is doing this," tagging it with a specific tag, and then you can'

[03:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tag the second person as "person two," and so on. You can add videos and label them as "video one " or "video two", and audio by labeling them as "audio one" or "audio two", and the model preserves their identity when creating the entire frame.'

[03:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Let\'s move on to the steps. I know most of you understand what steps are, but for those who are a little confused about their purpose: when the model generates a video, it starts with static noise and gradually cleans it up over several passes. And each such passage'

[04:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is a step. So you can imagine static noise in the first step, and with each subsequent iteration, the model takes that blurry image and cleans it up a little bit until it recognizes what\'s going on, adds context based on that'

[04:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'noise reduction, and then moves on to the next step. The basic H3 model requires about 20 steps, and each step takes time, so fewer steps means higher speed. Now, inside those blue nodes on the screen,'

[04:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we have Turbo LoRA. You see , we have quite a few of them, so I\'ll explain what they do. So, Turbo LoRA is a small additional file, usually up to 2 GB, that retrains the model to do the job, for example, in four or eight steps'

[04:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'instead of the basic 20. This is where the names "four-step" and " eight-step" come from. They indicate how many passes the model was created for. There are also accelerators in the form of attention modes (attention mechanisms). Attention modes are much'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more complex than Turbo LoRA, but to put it simply: at each step, the model compares each part of the image with every other part to decide what should be where. This comparison is called attention, and'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it is the most resource-intensive process performed by the model. And attention mode is just another way to perform these calculations faster, and you can choose from several popular modes. They are'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'popular because they speed up generation with minimal loss of quality. So, PyTorch attention, or SDPA, stands for scaled dot product attention . This is a standard'

[05:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'option built into PyTorch. If you\'ve run video generation in ComfyUI before, you\'ve probably already used it . This is the standard default option . It\'s accurate, it always works , and it\'s the baseline against which everything else is compared . Sage attention was the'

[05:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'next popular mode that appeared quite a while ago, and it actually performs calculations with less precision. It uses the int8 format instead of the full 16 bits. Therefore, it'

[05:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'processes less data. It\'s faster, and the only question is whether you\'ll see a visual difference in quality. These two green nodes here are newbies to this. Comfy kitchen attention is a custom optimized version from ComfyUI. It performs'

[06:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the same calculations, but better organized on your GPU. And SLA, or sparse linear attention, is an aggressive method. It doesn\'t compare every part of the image to every other. He decides which'

[06:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comparisons are important and skips the rest. Of course, this is a subjective decision, and if you skip too much, strange artifacts will appear. So this is a knob, not a switch, and I\'m using its default settings'

[06:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', but you can see a lot more options here, as the aggressiveness is adjustable. But by default, it does its job perfectly. So, before moving to any Turbo LoRA, we'

[06:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'test which of these attention modes provides the fastest generation. Of course, I do n\'t want to wait 345 seconds of generation again for a 5-second clip. So I'

[06:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
